llvm-args reach the driver, and a target's are passed as well

-C llvm-args reached cc1 only.  They now reach the GCC driver as well, so
a flag selecting a core variant (the C33's -mcore=c33pe) also reaches the
assembler, which records it in the object.

A target's llvm_args (llvm-args in a target spec) were not passed at all.
They now come first, ahead of -C llvm-args, which can override them, so
a target can carry the options its GCC's driver adds itself, such as the
C33 Linux driver's -mfdpic.

diff --git a/src/gcc_util.rs b/src/gcc_util.rs
index b0442cdf..fe7d318e 100644
--- a/src/gcc_util.rs
+++ b/src/gcc_util.rs
@@ -278,8 +278,13 @@ pub fn new_context<'gcc>(sess: &Session) -> Context<'gcc> {
 
     add_feature_options(&context, &sess.target.arch, &sess.global_backend_features);
 
-    for arg in &sess.opts.cg.llvm_args {
+    // NOTE: the driver gets them as well, so that a flag selecting a core variant
+    // (e.g. -mc33pe) also reaches the assembler, which records it in the object.
+    // The target's own arguments first, so that -C llvm-args can override them.
+    let target_args = sess.target.llvm_args.iter().map(|arg| arg.as_ref());
+    for arg in target_args.chain(sess.opts.cg.llvm_args.iter().map(String::as_str)) {
         context.add_command_line_option(arg);
+        context.add_driver_option(arg);
     }
     // NOTE: This is needed to compile the file src/intrinsic/archs.rs during a bootstrap of rustc.
     context.add_command_line_option("-fno-var-tracking-assignments");
